---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

// The prompt an assessment specialist will paste into FLO / ChatGPT / Codex so an
// AI's output imports into the (planned) Item Workbench cleanly. The file format
// is the contract; this prompt is how writers target it.
const itemPrompt = `You are writing assessment items in the Drawbridge item format.
Output a single Markdown file and nothing else.

Rules:
- Each item is an H2 heading with a short display code (e.g. ## MC-001).
- Do NOT invent an id comment; Drawbridge assigns ids on import.
- Under each item, use these H3 sections:
  ### Meta   (lines like  type: multiple_choice  /  status: draft  /  alignment: [co_xxx]  /  tags: [..])
  ### Stem   (the question prompt)
  ### Options  (a bullet list; omit for true_false)
  ### Answer   (the correct option text; multiple lines for multiple_answer; True/False for true_false)
  ### Rationale  (optional)
- Allowed types: multiple_choice, multiple_answer, true_false.
- Structure carries meaning. Do not use bold or asterisks to mark answers.
- Keep options plausible and parallel; avoid "all of the above".

Write [N] items aligned to the following outcomes: [paste outcome codes/text].`;
---

<Base title="Conventions">
  <h1>Conventions</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Drawbridge never runs an AI. Instead, each tool's file format is documented so
    any assistant — FLO, ChatGPT, Codex — can produce content that imports cleanly.
    The format is the contract; these pages are how you target it.
  </p>

  <h2>Outcomes <span class="tag">Ready</span></h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/outcomes">Outcome Builder</a> reads and writes the
    <code>drawbridge-outcomes/1</code> format. The
    <a href="/conventions/outcomes">outcomes convention</a> covers the structure, a
    downloadable starter template, and a copy-ready prompt for drafting a course's
    outcomes with an AI.
  </p>

  <h2>Items <span class="tag tag-planned">Planned</span></h2>
  <p>
    The Item Workbench isn't built yet. When it ships it will import Markdown in the
    convention below — one H2 per item with structured H3 sections — so AI-drafted
    banks arrive ready to review. Copy this, fill in the bracketed parts, and keep
    it for when the tool lands.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{itemPrompt}</code></pre>

  <h2>Format specifications</h2>
  <p>
    The authoritative, versioned specs live in the repository:
    <code>docs/formats/outcomes-format.md</code> (shipped) and
    <code>docs/formats/items-format.md</code> (planned).
  </p>

  <style>
    h1 { font-size: 1.9rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
    h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 2rem 0 0.5rem; }
    .lede { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--color-muted); max-width: 42rem; }
    .tag {
      font-size: 0.6em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      vertical-align: middle;
      color: var(--color-accent);
      border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
      border-radius: 999px;
      padding: 0.15em 0.55em;
      margin-left: 0.4rem;
    }
    .tag-planned {
      color: var(--color-muted);
      border-color: var(--color-line);
    }
    pre {
      background: var(--color-ink);
      color: #f4f2ec;
      padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem;
      border-radius: 10px;
      overflow-x: auto;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
    }
    pre code { font-family: var(--font-mono); white-space: pre-wrap; }
    p code {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.85em;
      background: var(--color-accent-soft);
      padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
      border-radius: 4px;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
